{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cfex6orruEd3A7mXwCsgd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe audit logic is correctly isolated in its own single-responsibility class (`AuditOwnerCreated`) with a dedicated step and good javadoc — that part is clean and additive. Two structural deviations from this codebase's idiom:\n\n1. **`govern: [ transaction ]` on a pure-logging step.** In this repo transaction governance marks steps that do transactional DB work (`save`, `apply`). `AuditOwnerCreated` only writes a log line — it performs no persistence — so carrying `govern: [ transaction ]` is misleading: it signals the step participates in the write transaction when it does not, and it holds the DB transaction open across logging I/O for no reason. Per the worked example, the responder step (`respond`) is intentionally un-governed; the audit step belongs in that same un-governed tail. Drop the `govern:` line from the `audit:` entry.\n\n2. **Generic method name `service`.** Every other function class in this pipeline names its single public method after its verb (`validate`, `load`, `apply`, `save`, `respond`). Rename `service(...)` to `audit(...)` to match the codebase's convention and the class/step name. OfficeFloor requires exactly one public method either way, so there's no wiring cost.\n\nNeither affects where the logic lives (the class placement is right) — they're idiom/consistency fixes so a maintainer reads the step the same way as its siblings."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9376,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9376},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":nul